An optical fibre is a very thin, flexible thread of transparent glass or plastic that guides light by total internal reflection. It consists of a core of high refractive index (about 1.50), a cladding of slightly lower refractive index (about 1.45) and a protective plastic jacket.
How information is carried: the electrical signal from a microphone or a digital source is applied to a light source, an LED or a semiconductor laser, whose intensity is modulated by the signal. The modulated light enters the core and travels along the fibre. At the core-cladding boundary a ray striking at an angle greater than the critical angle θ_c = sin⁻¹(n₂/n₁) is totally internally reflected, so it zig-zags along the fibre with very little loss (about 0.2 dB/km). At the receiving end a photodiode detects the light and converts it back into an electrical signal, which is amplified and demodulated.
Types: step-index multimode, graded-index multimode, in which rays follow curved paths and dispersion is reduced, and single-mode fibre with a core of a few micrometres, used for long distances. Repeaters or optical amplifiers are needed only every 50 to 100 km.
Advantages over other channels:
(a) enormous bandwidth, of the order of 10¹⁴ Hz, so thousands of telephone channels and many television programmes can be carried by one fibre;
(b) very low attenuation, hence long repeater spacing;
(c) complete immunity to electromagnetic interference and crosstalk, and very difficult to tap, which makes it secure;
(d) no electrical conduction, so no short-circuit or spark hazard and safe use in explosive atmospheres;
(e) light in weight, small in size, made from cheap silica instead of copper, and immune to corrosion;
(f) no earthing problems.
Its disadvantages are that installation and splicing need precision and special equipment, and the fibre is fragile if bent too sharply.
